        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "d2dc12c8-77e3-5912-8a73-5447e53108e7",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Products: Commodore 16, Commodore 116, Prológica CP-400, Proof of Destruction\n- Companies: Commodore International, Prológica, Mastertronic\n- People: Shaun Southern\n- Systems: Commodore 64, 6502-compatible 7501 CPU, 6502-compatible 8501 CPU\n- Places: Europe, Brazil\n- Concepts: entry-level computers, fixed shooters\n\nFacts:\n- In 1984, Commodore International released the Commodore 16 home computer as an entry-level replacement for the VIC-20.\n- The Commodore 16 used a 6502-compatible 7501 or 8501 CPU.\n- In 1984, Prológica introduced the CP-400 to the Brazilian market as part of its \"CP\" personal computer series.\n- Prológica was a Brazilian company that made its own versions of 8-bit US computers.\n- Commodore International sold the cost-reduced Commodore 116 only in Europe.\n- In 1987, Mastertronic published Proof of Destruction, a fixed shooter for the Commodore 64 and Commodore 16 written by Shaun Southern.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "4dda6508-e86b-5d03-a9dd-fd7e1d42a54f",
        "text": "Page: Commodore 16. Sentence: The Commodore 16 is a home computer made by Commodore International with a 6502-compatible 7501 or 8501 CPU, released in 1984 and intended to be an entry-level computer to replace the VIC-20.\nPage: Commodore 16. Sentence: A cost-reduced version, the Commodore 116, was sold only in Europe.\nPage: Prológica CP-400. Sentence: In the middle of 1984 a Brazilian company called Prológica, which made its own versions of 8 bits US computers, brought to the Brazilian market a new equipment for its personal computer series called \"CP\" (shorten of Personal Computer in Portuguese).\nPage: Proof of Destruction. Sentence: Proof of Destruction (P.O.D.) is a 1987 fixed shooter for the Commodore 64 and Commodore 16 written by Shaun Southern and published by Mastertronic.",
